gm-method (CLI oracle for GPU-vs-CPU parity + a probe-staleness trap that produces FALSE huge gaps): to test CPU-vs-GPU shader parity 'as many things as we need' from the CLI without manual browser-witness, launch headless chromium via CDP (auto-detect newest ms-playwright chromium chrome.exe; --use-angle=swiftshader for deterministic+portable software WebGL2, or =d3d11 for the real GPU), load the app, and batch-compare CPU fn(d) vs the GPU probe over a grid. CRITICAL TRAP: a pipelined GPU probe (sampleGroundM: clientWaitSync harvest + only issues a new read when none in flight, returns the LAST harvested value) is 1+ frames stale and CROSS-DIR contaminated - a naive 'poll until two equal reads' LOCKS ONTO A STALE PLATEAU from the PREVIOUS dir and reports FALSE gaps (witnessed 18m mean / 160m max that were pure artifact). The values even look like a plausible spatial SHIFT (cpu at point A ~= gpu at neighbor B), which falsely implicates a coordinate-mapping/faceWarp bug - a RED HERRING. FIX the drain: per dir, (1) warm-up burst (~8 rAF calls discarded) to flush the prior dir out of the pipe, then (2) require K>=4 CONSECUTIVE identical reads. After the robust drain the true gap collapsed to ~1.3m (the real float residual). LESSON: when a parity harness shows implausibly LARGE, spatially-coherent gaps, suspect probe staleness BEFORE a mapping bug; fix the drain and re-measure. A reliable CLI oracle also lets you A/B any height-rollout against ground truth repeatably.